<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Annals of the Joseon Dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1413">1413</a>–<a href="/years/1865">1865</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Joseon Wangjo Sillok</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Annals of the Joseon Dynasty (Joseon Wangjo Sillok) are the official chronicles of the reigns of Joseon kings from Taejo to Cheoljong, covering 1392 to 1863 in 1,893 volumes. Each reign's annals were compiled after the king's death from the daily notes of court historians, which kings were not allowed to read. Copies were stored in separate mountain archives for safety, and the annals were inscribed on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a>'s Memory of the World register in 1997.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/annals-of-the-joseon-dynasty.jpg" alt="Annals of the Joseon Dynasty" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Salamander724 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Joseon_Wangjo_Sillok_and_its_case_in_museum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The Annals of the Joseon Dynasty, called the Veritable Records or sillok, are state-compiled records of the reigns of the Joseon kings in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, kept from 1392. They run to 1,893 volumes and are thought to be the longest continual documentation of a single dynasty in the world. They are written in Classical Chinese. The records of the first three kings, Taejo, Jeongjong and Taejong, were handwritten; those of Sejong, who reigned from 1418 to 1450, and later kings were printed with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/movable-type">movable type</a>, wooden and metal, making Korea the first nation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a> to print its royal records this way. The sillok is designated the 151st national treasure of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-korea">South Korea</a> and is listed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a>'s Memory of the World register.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Eight historians of the seventh, eighth and ninth ranks in the Office of Royal Decrees kept daily records of court activities. They accompanied the king at all times, and the Joseon kings did not conduct official business without a historian present. These daily records, the sacho, were the basis of the annals, which a special committee of high-ranking officials and scholars compiled after a king's death. Three drafts were made, and the drafts, the sacho and the historians' private memoranda were then pulped in water to prevent leaks. Historians were guaranteed legal protection and editorial independence, and only they were allowed to read the sacho.</p><p>A story in the records of Taejong shows the principle. In the fourth year of his reign, equivalent to 18 March 1404 in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julian-calendar">Julian calendar</a>, the king fell from his horse while shooting at a deer and said, 'Do not let the historians know about this.' The historian present recorded both the fall and the order.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>From 1445 three additional copies were made and stored in Chungju, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeonju">Jeonju</a> and Seongju as well as in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seoul">Seoul</a>. During the Japanese invasions of 1592 to 1598 all but the Jeonju copy were destroyed, and the Jeonju records escaped fire only because scholars acted on their own initiative. Afterwards five repositories were kept, at the Ch'unch'ugwan, Mount Myohyang, Taebaeksan, Odaesan and Manisan. The Ch'unch'ugwan repository was destroyed in Yi Kwal's rebellion of 1624, and part of the Manisan collection was lost in the Qing invasion of 1636.</p><p>During the colonial period from 1910 to 1945 the Odaesan copies were taken to Tokyo Imperial University and mostly destroyed in the Great Kantō earthquake of 1923. The remaining 46 volumes were returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> in 2006, the year the National Institute of Korean History digitised the annals and put them online.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Veritable Records of the Joseon Dynasty” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Annals of the Joseon Dynasty</b> record 472 years of government day by day, written by court historians whose drafts even the king was not supposed to read.</blockquote><a href="/empire/joseon">Joseon · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
